(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-inflammation-quiz"]={"title":"Inflammation — Quiz 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the three linked vascular mechanisms?","choices":["Endothelial contraction, then exudation, then vasodilation of arterioles","Exudation, then vasodilation, then endothelial contraction","Vasodilation of arterioles, then endothelial contraction increasing capillary permeability, then exudation","Endothelial contraction, then vasodilation of the arterioles, then leukocyte transmigration into the tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vasodilation raises blood flow, endothelial contraction opens the capillaries, and fluid and blood proteins then move into the interstitial spaces. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which two cell types both act against virus-infected and tumor cells?","choices":["T helper cells and regulatory suppressor T cells","B lymphocytes and plasma cells","CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and natural killer cells","Monocytes and macrophages"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and natural killer cells are the two subsets that act against virus-infected and tumor cells. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"In the sequence of events of acute inflammation, which event immediately follows increased vascular permeability?","choices":["Vasoconstriction of the arterioles","Hemoconcentration and stasis","Chemotaxis","Phagocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hemoconcentration and stasis come directly after increased vascular permeability and directly before leukocyte adhesion. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why does stasis of blood flow matter mechanistically in acute inflammation?","choices":["It marks the point at which acute inflammation gives way to chronic inflammation in the tissue","It reverses the earlier increase in permeability","It terminates the inflammatory response","It precedes leukocyte adhesion, since slowed flow lets leukocytes contact the endothelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemoconcentration and stasis sit immediately before leukocyte adhesion in the sequence of events of acute inflammation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which movement does the term exudation describe?","choices":["Leukocytes from the interstitium back into the capillary lumen","Erythrocytes into the alveolar space","Fluid and blood proteins into the interstitial spaces","Fibrin into the vascular lumen to form a thrombus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exudation follows the increased capillary permeability produced by endothelial contraction. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which pairing of removal mechanism and result of failed removal is correct for the meshwork of fibrinous inflammation?","choices":["Phagocytosis by neutrophils; failure produces pus","Fibrolytic enzymes; failure produces scar tissue","Complement fragment C3b; failure results in ulceration","Macrophage digestion; failure results in gangrene"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The meshwork can only be removed by fibrolytic enzymes, and failure of this results in scar tissue formation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is characteristic of the exudate of acute inflammation?","choices":["The lymphocyte","The neutrophil","The plasma cell","The eosinophil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute inflammation involves exudation of fluid, plasma, proteins and leukocytes, and the leukocyte of that exudate is the neutrophil. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Histamine and serotonin share which pair of effects?","choices":["Chemotaxis and opsonization","Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability","Pain and fibrin deposition","Antibody production and antigen presentation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Histamine dilates arterioles and increases capillary and venule permeability, and serotonin likewise causes vasodilation and increased vascular permeability. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which feature defines granulocytes under light microscopy?","choices":["Differently staining cytoplasmic granules: membrane-bound digestive enzymes","A kidney-shaped nucleus that permits the cell to transmigrate between two adjacent endothelial cells nearby","Azurophilic granules that function purely as antigen-presenting structures","Surface receptors that bind the Fc portion of immunoglobulin G"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The granules are membrane-bound enzymes whose primary action is digesting what has been phagocytized. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which histological findings go with chronic inflammation?","choices":["Lymphocytes and macrophages, blood vessel proliferation, fibrosis and tissue necrosis","Neutrophil predominance with an absence of vascular proliferation","Eosinophil infiltration with mucus hypersecretion","Serous exudate with low protein content, no cellular infiltrate, and no proliferation of blood vessels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic inflammation is associated histologically with lymphocytes and macrophages, new blood vessels, fibrosis and tissue necrosis. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which pattern of inflammation is defined by mucus hypersecretion accompanying inflammation of a mucous membrane?","choices":["Serous","Hemorrhagic","Catarrhal","Pseudomembranous"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Catarrhal inflammation is mucus hypersecretion accompanying inflammation of a mucous membrane. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the fate of a monocyte once it leaves the bloodstream?","choices":["A tissue macrophage that clears debris and attacks microorganisms","A plasma cell that secretes antibody directly into the interstitium","A fibroblast that lays down scar tissue","A dendritic cell that migrates to the thymus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Monocytes eventually leave the bloodstream to become tissue macrophages with exactly these roles. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What do regulatory (suppressor) T cells do?","choices":["Producing antibody directed against antigens the host has encountered previously","Presenting phagocytosed material to naive T cells","Killing tumor cells directly through granule release","Returning immunity to normal after infection, and preventing autoimmunity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Regulatory (suppressor) T cells return the immune system to normal operation after infection and prevent autoimmunity. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Pseudomembranous inflammation consists of a superficial necrotic layer made up of which components?","choices":["Mucus, plasma and erythrocytes","Granulation tissue, proliferating fibroblasts and newly formed capillary networks throughout","Fibrin, inflammatory cells and debris, forming a membrane-like covering over the affected mucosa","Pus alone, without a fibrin component"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pseudomembranous inflammation is a superficial necrotic layer of fibrin, inflammatory cells and debris forming a membrane-like covering over mucosa. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Suppurative (purulent) inflammation contains which combination of material?","choices":["Fibrinogen forming a thick meshwork over the serosal surface","Low-protein plasma with minimal cellular content","A superficial necrotic layer forming a membrane over affected mucosa","Remnants of white blood cells, protein and tissue debris — that is, pus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Suppurative (purulent) inflammation contains remnants of white blood cells, protein and tissue debris, which is pus. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which combination of cause and relationship to other exudates characterizes hemorrhagic inflammation?","choices":["Mucus hypersecretion, and occurring in isolation","Fibrinogen deposition, and preceding every other exudate pattern","Epithelial sloughing, and replacing other exudates","Damage to blood vessels, and occurring together with other exudates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemorrhagic inflammation is damage to blood vessels occurring with other exudates. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A patient's inflamed tissue shows a leukocyte infiltrate dominated by cells with bi-lobed nuclei and pink-orange granules. Which process does this pattern most suggest?","choices":["An acute bacterial infection","An allergic or parasitic process","A chronic autoimmune process","A humoral antibody response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bi-lobed nuclei with pink-orange granules identify eosinophils, which predominate in allergic reactions and parasitic infections. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why can a neutrophil kill only a limited number of pathogens before dying?","choices":["It cannot renew its lysosomes, so dies after phagocytosing a few","It lacks the enzymes needed to digest the bacterial cell wall it engulfs","It must first be opsonized before it can engulf anything","It converts into a macrophage after a single encounter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are not able to renew their lysosomes, so they die after having phagocytosed a few pathogens. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is inflammation?","choices":["The reaction of vascularized living tissue to injury","The proliferation of fibroblasts following any cellular insult","The activation of the adaptive immune system by a foreign antigen","The death of tissue through coagulative or liquefactive pathways"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inflammation is defined as the reaction of vascularized living tissue to injury, so living tissue with a blood supply is part of the definition. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Vasodilation of the arterioles supplying an injured region produces which immediate effect?","choices":["Reduced hydrostatic pressure within the capillary bed","Contraction of the capillary endothelium","Movement of blood proteins into the interstitium","Increased blood flow to the injured region"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vasodilation of the supplying arterioles increases blood flow. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which cell types characterize chronic inflammation?","choices":["Neutrophils and eosinophils predominantly, without mononuclear infiltrate","Macrophages, monocytes, lymphocytes and plasma cells","Basophils and mast cells only","Fibroblasts in the absence of leukocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic inflammation is characterized by macrophages, monocytes and mononuclear cells, with proliferation of blood vessels and connective tissue. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the cellular events of acute inflammation after stasis?","choices":["Leukocyte adhesion, transmigration, chemotaxis, aggregation, phagocytosis","Chemotaxis, phagocytosis, adhesion, transmigration, aggregation","Transmigration, adhesion, aggregation, phagocytosis, chemotaxis","Aggregation, chemotaxis, transmigration, adhesion, phagocytosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adhesion to the endothelium precedes movement across it, then directed migration, gathering at the site, and finally ingestion. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which pairing of response and mediator describes the basophil's chief role?","choices":["Parasitic defense, through major basic protein","Antibody production, through immunoglobulin G","Cell-mediated immunity, through T cell receptors","Allergic and antigen response, through histamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Basophils drive allergic and antigen response via histamine, which causes inflammation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which combination of granule color and nuclear shape identifies the eosinophil?","choices":["Pale lilac granules, multilobed nucleus","Coarse dark granules, bi- or tri-lobed nucleus","Pink-orange granules, bi-lobed nucleus","No visible granules, kidney-shaped nucleus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cytoplasm is full of granules that assume a characteristic pink-orange color, with a bi-lobed nucleus. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why can an implanted medical device provoke an inflammatory response even when it is sterile?","choices":["Because sterilization leaves residual endotoxin behind, and that endotoxin then acts as a chemotactic agent","Because it becomes coated with immunoglobulin G and C3b, which immune cells recognize","Because the device physically obstructs lymphatic drainage","Because implantation always causes bacterial contamination of surrounding tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Opsonization of the biomaterial is what makes it a target regardless of sterility. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"The term PMN (polymorphonuclear leukocyte) refers to which cells?","choices":["All granulocytes","Neutrophils only","All mononuclear leukocytes","Plasma cells and memory B cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphonuclear leukocyte, abbreviated PMN, is used to refer to all granulocytes rather than to neutrophils alone. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What effects does serotonin have?","choices":["Pain generation and leukocyte chemotaxis","Opsonization of biomaterials","Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability","Formation of a fibrous network trapping microorganisms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Serotonin causes vasodilation and increased vascular permeability, the same pair of vascular effects as histamine. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"In the five main processes of the inflammatory response, what immediately follows the migration of neutrophils?","choices":["Increased blood flow","Chemotaxis","Increased permeability","Exudation of plasma proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The order runs increased blood flow, increased permeability, migration of neutrophils, chemotaxis, then leukocyte recruitment and activation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What distinguishes dry from wet gangrene?","choices":["Dry involves fibrinous exudate; wet involves serous exudate","Dry is coagulative necrosis; wet is liquefactive necrosis arising from infection","Dry gangrene occurs on mucosal surfaces; wet gangrene occurs on serosal surfaces","Dry is acute; wet is chronic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gangrenous inflammation is a severe form of necrosis with or without superimposed bacterial infection, dry being coagulative and wet liquefactive from infection. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which two mediators are plasma proteins?","choices":["Bradykinin and complement components","Histamine and serotonin","Immunoglobulin G and C3b","Fibrinogen and fibrolytic enzymes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bradykinin and complement components are both plasma proteins, as is the coagulation system. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
